\description{
Turn phrases, spaCy-style pattern lists or a data.frame of patterns into a
\code{spacyr_entity_patterns} object: a checked set of rules for spaCy's
\code{entity_ruler}, which finds named entities by matching phrases or sequences
of tokens. Creating the object needs neither Python nor spaCy; pass it to
\code{\link[=spacy_initialize]{spacy_initialize()}} as \code{entity_patterns} to add the rules to the pipeline.
}
\details{
Token patterns are checked for their structure only: that each is a list
with one named list per token. spaCy checks the attribute names, operators
and values when the patterns are added to a pipeline, and reports any
errors then.

Two conversions make patterns from R reach spaCy in the form it expects.
The values of spaCy's set operators (\code{IN}, \code{NOT_IN}, \code{IS_SUBSET},
\code{IS_SUPERSET} and \code{INTERSECTS}) are always stored as lists, so that
\code{list(LOWER = list(IN = "dr"))} stays a list of one value, rather than
becoming a single string. Whole numbers stored as doubles, such as \code{3}
in \code{list(LENGTH = 3)}, become integers, since spaCy does not accept
decimal numbers in token patterns.

Phrases, including dictionary values without wildcards, are split into
tokens by the pipeline's own tokenizer, and some of its rules depend on
case: spaCy's English models keep \code{"Inc."} as one token, but split
\code{"inc."} into \code{"inc"} and \code{"."}. So write phrases in their usual case, even with
\code{case_insensitive = TRUE}, and make dictionaries with
\code{quanteda::dictionary(tolower = FALSE)}, since by default it lowercases
their values.

With \code{case_insensitive = TRUE}, the parts of a phrase or dictionary value
that has a wildcard are put in lower case by R when the patterns are
made, since making them does not need spaCy, while spaCy compares them
with its own lower case form of each token. The two differ for a few
characters, such as the dotted capital I of Turkish, and a value with
such a character and a wildcard may then not match. This holds wherever
the patterns are used, in \code{\link[=spacy_initialize]{spacy_initialize()}} or in \code{\link[=spacy_match]{spacy_match()}}.
Given the character vector or the dictionary itself, and not patterns
made from it, \code{\link[=spacy_match]{spacy_match()}} lowercases with spaCy and does not have
this limit.

Patterns match spaCy's tokens, not characters. For instance, spaCy's
English models make "Prof." a single token, so a pattern for academic
titles needs to list both \code{"prof"} and \code{"prof."}, to match titles written
with and without a full stop.
}
\examples{
# phrases, labelled by their names or by `label`
as_entity_patterns(c(ORG = "Acme Widgets", GPE = "San Francisco"))
as_entity_patterns(c("Acme Widgets", "Globex"), label = "ORG")

# phrases with wildcards, in any case
as_entity_patterns(c(ORG = "acme*", ORG = "globex corp*"),
                   valuetype = "glob", case_insensitive = TRUE)

# a token pattern for people with academic titles: a title, followed by
# the first token of a person the model recognises (ENT_IOB "B") and the
# rest of that person (ENT_IOB "I"), so that it extends one entity without
# running into a person who follows it
titles <- c("dr", "dr.", "prof", "prof.")
as_entity_patterns(list(
  label = "PERSON",
  pattern = list(list(LOWER = list(IN = titles)),
                 list(ENT_TYPE = "PERSON", ENT_IOB = "B"),
                 list(ENT_TYPE = "PERSON", ENT_IOB = "I", OP = "*"))
))

# a data.frame of patterns
as_entity_patterns(data.frame(label = c("ORG", "ORG"),
                              pattern = c("Acme Widgets", "Globex")))

# a quanteda dictionary, with labels from its keys
if (requireNamespace("quanteda", quietly = TRUE)) {
  dict <- quanteda::dictionary(list(ORG = c("acme widgets", "globex*"),
                                    PERSON = "dr * who"))
  as_entity_patterns(dict)

  # labels from the first two levels of its keys
  nested <- quanteda::dictionary(list(
    place = list(us = c("new york", "boston"), uk = "london")))
  as_entity_patterns(nested)
  as_entity_patterns(nested, levels = 1:2)
}

# entities found by spaCy, to use as rules for other texts
\dontrun{
spacy_initialize()
found <- spacy_extract_entity("Acme Widgets hired Jane Smith.")
as_entity_patterns(found)
spacy_finalize()
}

# combine sets of patterns
c(as_entity_patterns(c(ORG = "Globex")),
  as_entity_patterns(c(PERSON = "Jane Smith")))
}
